{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "page_meta", "target_id": "p0040", "field": "review_status", "before": "draft", "after": "locked", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_001", "field": "kind", "before": "citation", "after": "gloss", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_001", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "This is Ussher's own Latin translation of the Greek phrase about Paul (τὸν τὴν οἰκουμένην σταδεύσαντα...), placed in the footnote rather than the body. An interesting editorial choice — the Greek stays in the main text, the Latin crib goes below.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_002", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": " locates Jerome's commentary on Amos", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_003", "field": "kind", "before": "citation", "after": "gloss", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_003", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "identifies the Psalm being alluded to in Jerome's quotation (\"from the highest heaven his going forth\"). This is more gloss than citation — Jerome doesn't name the Psalm explicitly, so Ussher supplies the reference for the reader.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_004", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "three separate authorities all cited together for the claim about Paul preaching in the West after his first defence. Ussher is showing the tradition's breadth.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_005", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "Scripture reference for Paul's words quoted in the body", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_006", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "locates Theodoret's commentary on Psalm 116 for the \"islands in the sea\" addition", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_007", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": " locates the Venantius Fortunatus couplet. Worth flagging because the verse itself is doing heavy rhetorical lifting in the body — \"He crossed the Ocean... the lands the Briton holds... farthest Thule\" — and the footnote simply locates it without comment.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:45:28.595254+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "footnote", "target_id": "p0040_fn_008", "field": "notes", "before": "", "after": "three modern/early modern authorities stacked together — the Magdeburg Centuriators, Robert Persons, and John Pits. Notable because these are secondary literature rather than patristic sources — Ussher is citing the scholarly conversation about Paul in Britain.", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
{"ts": "2026-05-02T17:46:09.961675+00:00", "page_id": "p0040", "scope": "line", "target_id": "p0040_header_l0001", "field": "text_gold", "before": "CAP. I.", "after": "CAP. I. ANTIQUITATES. 19", "reviewer": "Paul Boys"}
